  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 522 PM MDT Sat Oct 3 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThe upper-level ridge will continue its slow eastward movement, \nallowing for an extended period of dry and warm conditions for \nMontana. A weak shortwave on Monday will cause breezy winds in \ncentral and northern MT with the breeziest winds impacting the \nNorthern Rocky Mountain Front. Due to lower RH values (<25% for most \nof the region) occurring at the same time as the breezy winds, \nthere will be the potential for areas of elevated fire weather \nconcerns.\n\nAs the upper-level ridge builds back up, there will be areas of \nbreezy westerly winds on Tuesday. Beyond that, the upper-level ridge \nwill keep winds mild across the region through Thursday. Although \ntemperatures remain unseasonably warm, there will be a cool off of \naround 5-10 degrees on Tuesday. Temperatures then remain fairly \nconsistent through at least Thursday as the upper-level ridge \ncontinues to impact the region.\n\nAlthough the temperatures continue to be warm on Friday, it looks \nlike it could be the transition day for a pattern shift in the \nweather due to an upper-level low developing over the PNW. \nConditions then look wetter and cooler during the weekend, with even \nthe potential for some minor mountain snow accumulations. However, \nconfidence still remains low in this scenario due to the amount of \ntime until Friday and the weekend. - ESH\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThere is high confidence in temperatures reaching the low 80s across \nmany lower elevation locations on Monday with some locations, \nincluding Great Falls and Helena, expected to get within 5 degrees \nof the daily record. -thor\n\nFor elevated fire weather concerns in north and central Montana \nMonday afternoon, there is a 70% chance or greater for sustained \nwinds greater than or equal to 15 mph and RH (relative humidity) \nvalues of less than or equal to 25%. However, due to fuel condition \nnot being critical, products are not being considered at this time. -\nesh\n\nRegarding the potential pattern shift next weekend, there have been \nsome runs of both the GFS and ECMWF that have pointed towards \nmountain snow with an occasional run suggesting even some lower \nelevation snow in Southwest Montana. Because there is still a bit of \na \"windshield wiper\" effect going on between model runs, with one \nrun being more robust on the snow potential and the next one taking \nit away, it is highly recommended that folks take any mention of \nsnow with a grain of salt until we get closer to next weekend. A \npattern shift towards cooler and wetter conditions is possible, but \nconfidence in any one scenario is low. -thor",
